Docker regression verification completed on October 3, 2026, against clean `main` commit [`3511ba49514fe8cf525f5a22c16c3806bf3886ba`](https://github.com/mario-andreschak/FLUJO/commit/3511ba49514fe8cf525f5a22c16c3806bf3886ba).

The two targeted fixes in [`0c9506a9`](https://github.com/mario-andreschak/FLUJO/commit/0c9506a9ba97bd7d3829cb248307b4254c199f1e) are verified: capped ephemeral children are released, and the AtlasCloud-style HTTP 400 wrapper no longer triggers the custom retry loop.

| Measurement | Current main | Same main with only those fixes reverted |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Regression tests | 29 passed, 0 failed | 23 passed, 6 failed as expected |
| Children executed | 96 | 96 |
| Capped child states still retained | 0 after every batch | 5 more per batch; 60 at the end |
| Post-GC retained heap growth | -2.64 MiB (no net growth) | +253.25 MiB |
| Requests for each injected provider HTTP 400 | 1 | 3 |
| Peak container memory | 858.28 MiB | 1066.05 MiB |
| OOM / container exit | No OOM; exit 0 | No OOM; exit 1 from regression assertions |

The full workload comprised 12 batches of eight children, with four executing concurrently and four queued. Each batch had five capped, two normally completed and one failed child. The first four lanes were three capped children plus one failed child. A 2,000,000-character parent stayed live throughout; each child had 3,600,000–5,600,000 characters of independently generated transcript content. At four characters per sizing token, that represents the reported ~500k parent / ~900k–1.4M child range; this is an explicit sizing proxy, not a tokenizer measurement. No state-map/cache clearing occurred between batches. Archive files were deleted after each batch solely to bound test disk usage.

The harness exercises production `runFlow`, `runSubflowLanes`, conversation-cache bookkeeping, `OpenAiAdapter`, the actual OpenAI SDK HTTP transport, and real archive/outcome reads and writes. Graph/node execution is a deterministic test double; the ModelHandler dispatch-cloning workload is reproduced through real adapter hooks rather than invoking ModelHandler itself. The local HTTP fixture returns the AtlasCloud-style 400 error wrapper. Current main verified all 158 archived dispatch outcomes. A separate persisted-conversation test also failed once with HTTP 400, then resumed successfully with the original history preserved and one request for each turn.

All test processes ran in separate Linux Docker containers on Node v22.23.2: 6 GiB memory and memory+swap limits (no additional swap), 2 CPUs, 256 PIDs, a 4096 MiB V8 old-space limit, `--network=none`, no exposed ports and no host mounts. The provider was loopback-only inside each container. Dependencies were installed from the pinned commit's lockfile before disabling networking. Neither the existing checkout's uncommitted changes nor real credentials/runtime data were used.

**Assessment:** the two diagnosed regressions are fixed and the negative control demonstrates that the harness detects them. This was a controlled large-context regression test, not a replay against the original live provider or a reproduction of the fatal 4 GiB crash. It does not establish bounds for all live/paused states, media payloads, event retention or every archival allocation. I am leaving #520 open pending confirmation of the original production workload; this comment records the verified fix evidence without treating broader OOM behavior as proven resolved.

The existing regression suites are [`gracefulCapLanding.test.ts`](https://github.com/mario-andreschak/FLUJO/blob/3511ba49514fe8cf525f5a22c16c3806bf3886ba/__tests__/flow/gracefulCapLanding.test.ts), [`transientRetry.test.ts`](https://github.com/mario-andreschak/FLUJO/blob/3511ba49514fe8cf525f5a22c16c3806bf3886ba/__tests__/model/transientRetry.test.ts), and [`modelTurnArchive.test.ts`](https://github.com/mario-andreschak/FLUJO/blob/3511ba49514fe8cf525f5a22c16c3806bf3886ba/__tests__/flow/modelTurnArchive.test.ts). The additional harness and exact two-hunk reversion are included below for reproduction.

